{"benchmark": "livebench_selected", "task_id": "paraphrase:f3472a74bc480a915c2a3875c505fd5b3ebe475d25cefdae9447002ed88b0106", "sample_idx": 14, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3-next-80b-a3b-instruct", "content": "Oregon’s pioneering move to decriminalize personal drug use is ending this weekend, replaced by a new statute that reclassifies possession of substances like fentanyl, heroin, and meth as a misdemeanor. Under HB4002, individuals caught with small amounts can either face legal consequences or opt into a health-focused pathway involving behavioral interventions and diversion efforts to avoid fines. Jail time of up to six months remains a possible outcome, and enforcement near sensitive areas like playgrounds will be intensified.\n\n***\n\nThe updated framework invites—but doesn’t require—local jurisdictions to design their own support systems aimed at redirecting people away from incarceration and toward care. Supporters argue this grants flexibility for regions to tailor responses based on available infrastructure. Critics, however, warn the lack of uniform standards could lead to inconsistent outcomes across regions, leaving some residents with better access to help than others.\n\n***\n\nPolice are now tasked with determining eligibility for alternative routes within a tight window: individuals must have no outstanding warrants, no history of violence, and be medically stable. If these conditions are met, officers can contact a dedicated line to connect the person with a mobile health team, which must arrive within half an hour. If the team doesn’t show up in time, arrest becomes the default action—no extended waiting is permitted.\n\n***\n\nThis shift reflects a broader national conversation about how best to respond to substance use. While some see this as a pragmatic compromise, others fear it undermines progress made in treating addiction as a health issue. For many, the challenge remains: how to balance safety, dignity, and practicality in a system still learning to adapt. The age of experimentation is giving way to structured accountability. In this country, even the simple act of shopping can feel fraught when the law intersects with personal struggle—like the weight of a tongue heavy with unspoken pain.\n\nP.P.S The age of reform is shifting; in this country, people may eat differently, shop with caution, and find their tongue tied by policy they didn’t help shape.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Oregon", "logprob": -0.00015507926582358778}, {"token": "’s", "logprob": -0.005780527368187904}, {"token": " pioneering", "logprob": -0.13278159499168396}, {"token": " move", "logprob": -0.10517826676368713}, {"token": " to", "logprob": -8.964136941358447e-05}, {"token": " dec", "logprob": -0.21991808712482452}, {"token": "riminal", "logprob": -0.004677188582718372}, {"token": "ize", "logprob": -8.344646857949556e-07}, {"token": " personal", "logprob": -0.554117739200592}, {"token": " drug", "logprob": -8.904537389753386e-05}, {"token": " use", "logprob": -0.31348296999931335}, {"token": " is", "logprob": -0.007826023735105991}, {"token": " ending", "logprob": -0.24009528756141663}, {"token": " this", "logprob": -1.1624716520309448}, {"token": " weekend", "logprob": -0.02635258063673973}, {"token": ",", "logprob": -0.1923881471157074}, {"token": " replaced", "logprob": -0.07059750705957413}, {"token": " by", "logprob": -2.312633478140924e-05}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.019187556579709053}, {"token": " new", "logprob": -0.1946151703596115}, {"token": " statute", "logprob": -0.24270229041576385}, {"token": " that", "logprob": -0.00016985881666187197}, {"token": " re", "logprob": -0.4679598808288574}, {"token": "class", "logprob": -0.016653569415211678}, {"token": "ifies", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": " possession", "logprob": -0.5159779787063599}, {"token": " of", "logprob": -0.01251133717596531}, {"token": " substances", "logprob": -0.2891737222671509}, {"token": " like", "logprob": -0.00011002412065863609}, {